  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh temperatures today will be early in the day as a cold \nfront is going to pass through mid morning into early afternoon.\nThe front is already to western NY early this morning and \nprogressing east at about 20 to 25 mph. The coastal low that \nmissed our region is moving into the Gulf of Maine and as it \ndoes, it will help the front to accelerate over the next few \nhours. There is not much precipitable water in place ahead of \nthe front but still a few sprinkles are likely as the front \nmoves through this morning. \n\n850 mb temperatures fall quickly behind the front with surface \ntemperatures gradually falling mid morning through the evening. \nAfter around 6 pm, the 850 mb temperatures get cold enough for \nsome lake effect precipitation to form. Initially, it will be \nwarm enough this evening for rain or a rain snow mix but \ntonight, precipitation will change to snow. The vertical wind \nprofile is not favorable for any organized banding off the Great\nLakes so the lake effect snow will likely be more scattered \nsnow showers. Snow accumulations are not expected for most of \nthe area though a few higher elevations in the Finger Lakes and \nCNY may get a dusting to half inch on elevated surfaces. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nModels continue to be in good agreement with the late week low \nforming off the Southeast Coast then moving north up through the\nAppalachian Mountains into the Northeast. The low is developing\nat the nose of a subtropical jet streak with a neutral to \nslightly negatively tilted trough in the central US. The trough \ndoes not contain a lot of cold air. Deep, subtropical moisture \nwraps into the the developing low on the east and northeast \nside. This warm air will be advecting into the Northeast well \nahead of the low, with SE 850 mb flow developing by Wednesday \nnight. 850 mb temperatures may make a run for around 10C by the \ntime the precipitation begins to arrive so the potential for any\nmixed precipitation at onset is gone so rain is the likely \nprecipitation type for this entire storm. \n\nHigher winds have potential with a strong low level jet moving \nin on the North side of the low as the precipitation moves in. \nForecast soundings have winds at 3000 feet above the surface \napproaching 60 to 70 mph. Forecast soundings are very stable in \nthe low levels so the ability to transfer some of that momentum \ndown is greatly limited. BUFKIT soundings only indicate wind \ngusts of 20 to 30 mph reaching the surface through the stable \nlayer so odds are we stay largely below wind advisory criteria \nbut will be something to watch. The peaks of the Catskills may \nsee some of those higher winds though. \n\nAs the low passes through New York Friday into Saturday, it \nbegins to occlude and becomes stacked. The rain will become more\nshowery in nature with winds decreasing significantly. One of \nthe model trends overnight has been to much less cold air \nwrapping into the occluding low from the Great Lakes trough so \neven after cold air advection begins, precipitation likely does \nnot change over to snow. The exception may be higher elevations \nin Steuben or the Catskills above 2000 feet.",
